second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"What is the historical period associated with the John Mason House?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 156, \"text\": \"John Mason House (Lexington, Massachusetts) The John Mason House is an historic First Period house on 1303 Massachusetts Avenue in Lexington, Massachusetts.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 157, \"end\": 316, \"text\": \"It is a 2-1/2 story wood frame structure, its main block three bays wide, with a side gable roof, clapboard siding, and two chimneys set behind the roof ridge.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 317, \"end\": 406, \"text\": \"A leanto section to the rear gives it a saltbox profile, and an ell extends to the right.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 407, \"end\": 500, \"text\": \"The oldest portion of the house, a three-bay portion of its main block, was built circa 1715.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 501, \"end\": 570, \"text\": \"The building has been repeatedly extended and altered over the years.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"start\": 571, \"end\": 586, \"text\": \"John Mason, its\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 53, \"text\": \"and 1871 this house was purchased by George Thurlow .\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 54, \"end\": 132, \"text\": \"This property remained in the Thurlow family until the mid-twentieth century .\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 133, \"end\": 177, \"text\": \"NWB.571 Benjamin Coker House , 172 State St.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 178, \"end\": 361, \"text\": \"Newburyport MA c 1700 The Benjamin Coker House embodies in its roof frame and second story tie beam , clear evidence of a distinctive First Period plan type and construction methods .\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 362, \"end\": 449, \"text\": \"While the house is undocumented before 1719 , the style and construction suggest a ca .\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 450, \"end\": 461, \"text\": \"1700 date .\"}, \"D2S7\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d01\", \"start\": 462, \"end\": 552, \"text\": \"The house began as a single cell , two story house , located at High and Federal Streets .\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 120, \"text\": \"John Chipman , who built the present house to accommodate his parish duties and his growing family of fifteen children .\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d02\", \"start\": 121, \"end\": 448, \"text\": \"The Exercise Conant House is the First Period , northern ell of the two-and-a-half story , five-bay Georgian house built by the Reverend John Chipman House , which has a dentilled cornice and an original fine Second-Period , central doorway with fluted pilasters and a broken-arch pediment with pineapple finial in the center .\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d02\", \"start\": 449, \"end\": 599, \"text\": \"The First Period Conant ell is a single-cell , two-and-a-half story structure , three bays wide , possibly originally oriented north , with chimney to\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 137, \"text\": \"John Mason House (Winchester, Massachusetts) The John Mason House is a historic house at 10 Hillside Avenue in Winchester, Massachusetts.\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"start\": 138, \"end\": 277, \"text\": \"This two story wood frame house was built sometime in the 1860s, probably for Joshua Stone, who sold it to John Mason sometime before 1875.\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"start\": 278, \"end\": 372, \"text\": \"Mason was one of the first Boston businessmen to establish a suburban residence in Winchester.\"}, \"D4S4\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"start\": 373, \"end\": 597, \"text\": \"The house has a variety of high-style Italianate features, including a characteristic low-pitch hip roof with decorative brackets, and a three-bay front facade in which paired narrow windows are topped by decorative framing.\"}, \"D4S5\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"start\": 598, \"end\": 631, \"text\": \"The front entry is sheltered by a\"}, \"D5S1\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 7, \"text\": \"John W.\"}, \"D5S2\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 8, \"end\": 31, \"text\": \"Mason House The John W.\"}, \"D5S3\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 32, \"end\": 117, \"text\": \"Mason House is a historic building located in Fergus Falls, Minnesota, United States.\"}, \"D5S4\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 118, \"end\": 195, \"text\": \"Mason was a native of Lapeer, Michigan and one of the first settlers in town.\"}, \"D5S5\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 196, \"end\": 243, \"text\": \"He arrived in 1871 to establish a law practice.\"}, \"D5S6\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 244, \"end\": 362, \"text\": \"He served in the Minnesota Legislature, he was the first mayor of Fergus Falls, and later served as its city attorney.\"}, \"D5S7\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 363, \"end\": 454, \"text\": \"Mason built a small home on this same lot in 1875, and replaced it with this house in 1881.\"}, \"D5S8\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d04\", \"start\": 455, \"end\": 551, \"text\": \"It is a two-story wood frame structure that features a limestone foundation, low pitch hip roof,\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"John Mason House\", \"scope\": \"historical period\", \"claim\": \"The John Mason House is associated with the First Period.\", \"answer\": \"First Period\", \"quote\": \"John Mason House (Lexington, Massachusetts) The John Mason House is an historic First Period house on 1303 Massachusetts Avenue in Lexington, Massachusetts.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0497-da80b4c9eb42:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"John Mason House\", \"scope\": \"historical period\", \"claim\": \"The historical period associated with the John Mason House is the 1860s.\", \"answer\": \"1860s\", \"quote\": \"This two story wood frame house was built sometime in the 1860s, probably for Joshua Stone, who sold it to John Mason sometime before 1875.\", \"original_document_number\": 4}], \"R5_answers\": [\"First Period\", \"1860s\", \"circa 1715\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"First Period\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"1860s\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C2\", \"answer\": \"circa 1715\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C3\", \"answer\": \"1700s (First Period)\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C4\", \"answer\": \"mid-twentieth century\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C5\", \"answer\": \"1881\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}]}"
